{start_of_verse label="Verse 1"}
Last [Em]night as I lay [G]dreaming
Of [D]pleasant days gone [Em]by,
My [Em]mind being bent on [G]rambling,
To [D]Ireland I did [Em]fly;
I [G]stepped on board a [D]vision
And I [C]followed with my [D]will,
And [Em]shortly came to [G]anchor
At the [D]cross of Spancil [Em]Hill.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 2"}
It [Em]being the twenty-[G]third of June,
The [D]day before the [Em]fair,
When [Em]Ireland's sons and [G]daughters
In [D]crowds assembled [Em]there;
The [G]young, the old, the [D]brave and bold
Came their [C]duty to ful[D]fil,
At the [Em]parish church in [G]Clooney,
A [D]mile from Spancil [Em]Hill.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 3"}
I [Em]went to see my [G]neighbours
To [D]hear what they might [Em]say;
The [Em]old ones were all [G]dead and gone,
The [D]young ones turning [Em]grey.
I [G]met with tailor [D]Quigley,
He's as [C]bold as ever [D]still;
Sure he [Em]used to make my [G]britches
When I [D]lived in Spancil [Em]Hill.
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse label="Verse 4"}
I [Em]dreamt I hugged and [G]kissed her
As [D]in the days of [Em]yore;
She said, '[Em]Johnny, you're only [G]joking,
As [D]many's the time be[Em]fore.'
The [G]cock he crew in the [D]morning,
He [C]crew both loud and [D]shrill,
And I a[Em]woke in Cali[G]fornia,
Many [D]miles from Spancil [Em]Hill.
{end_of_verse}
{x_end: Em}
